  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"A statement about the storage of nuclear waste in plastic. The line drawings are derived from children's textbooks, redrawn to show a disrupted nature. The toxic wastes, listed partially alphabetically, are printed on both sides of the embossed paper, each page separated by a plastic sheet. The unstable and hence inappropriate and hazardous use of plastic to contain toxic waste is emphasized by the diverse deformations of each of the plastic sheets"--Artist's statement from Vamp &amp; Tramp website.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"The plastics used in this book are the acrylic slipcase and illusion polycarbonate and flexible vinyl from Rowland Technologies in Connecticut; the papers are Barcham Green Cambersand and Cairo from Hayle Mill in England. Computer preparation by Ellen Dorn Levitt with printing by Andrew Miller-Brown and binding executed by Audrey Holden"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Resource for images: Hampson, B. ... 1962, Science from the beginning. Edinburgh, Oliver &amp; Boyd, Reource for text: Hasan, Syed, 1996, Geology and hazardous waste management. New Jersey, Prentice Hall"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In a non-adhesive binding with silver-colored paper holding six gatherings of two, all interleaved with translucent plastic sheets.  In an unfastened plastic wrapper and an acrylic slipcase.</subfield>
  </datafield>
